course: french
lesson: 5
title: Tu or vous? Manners in French
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: French has two words for "you": tu, the friendly one, and vous, the polite one, and choosing between them is part of good manners.
explain: Tu is for one friend, a child, your family or a pet; vous is for a grown-up you don't know well, a teacher, a waiter, and also for any group of people.
explain: Picture Archie at the school gate saying "Salut, tu vas bien ?" to his French teacher: it sounds as cheeky as calling her "mate".
explain: Many little phrases change with tu and vous: s'il te plaît for a friend, s'il vous plaît for a grown-up; à tes souhaits and à vos souhaits after a sneeze.
explain: When in doubt, use vous: a grown-up will tell you if they want you to say tu.
fact: tu ("tu") is the friendly you: for one friend, a child, family and pets.
fact: vous ("voo") is the polite you for a grown-up you don't know well, and it is also the you for more than one person, even friends.
fact: Vous allez bien ? means "Are you well?" said politely; Tu es très mignon ! means "You're very cute!" to a pet or a baby.
fact: Vous voulez jouer ? means "Do you (all) want to play?" to a group.
fact: s'il vous plaît ("seel voo PLAY") is the polite please; s'il te plaît ("seel tuh PLAY") is please to a friend.
fact: Merci ! means thank you and Merci beaucoup ! means thanks a lot; the answer De rien ! ("duh RYAN") means "you're welcome", literally "of nothing".
fact: Je vous en prie is a more polite way to say "you're welcome".
fact: Pardon ! means sorry or excuse me when you bump into someone or squeeze past; Excusez-moi ! is the polite "excuse me" to ask for help.
fact: Bonjour, madame and Bonjour, monsieur ("muh-SYUH") are the normal polite greetings, used by children too.
fact: Je veux means "I want" and can sound like a toddler demanding; Je voudrais ("zhuh voo-DRAY") means "I would like" and is the polite way to ask.
fact: In France people usually wait until everyone is served and someone says Bon appétit ! before starting to eat.
fact: When someone sneezes, say À vos souhaits ! to a grown-up or À tes souhaits ! to a friend; the person answers Merci !
fact: Mademoiselle is an old word for miss; today madame is the safe word for any grown-up woman, and official forms no longer use mademoiselle.
fact: Slang here: "my lover" (West Country) is a friendly way to call someone dear; "lowkey" means a bit, secretly; "bruv" means mate; "thanks heaps" (Australia) means thanks a lot; "like" at the end of a Belfast question just adds a friendly tone.
exercise: Which you is polite, for a grown-up you don't know well? | vous | tu | toi | ton
exercise: Archie says to his teacher, "Salut, tu vas bien ?" What should he have said? | Bonjour, madame, vous allez bien ? | Coucou, ma belle, tu vas bien ce matin ? | Salut, ça va, toi ? | Bonne nuit, tu vas bien ?
exercise: Emma says to her cousin's dog, "Vous êtes très mignon !" What should she say? | Tu es très mignon ! | Vous êtes très mignonne ! | Toi êtes très mignon ! | Vous es très mignon !
exercise: Amir asks three French kids to play. Which question fits? | Vous voulez jouer ? | Tu veux jouer avec moi ? | Toi veux jouer ? | Tu voulez jouer, les amis ?
exercise: Poppy orders from the waiter. How should she say please? | s'il vous plaît | s'il te plaît | merci beaucoup, mon pote | de rien
exercise: Logan thanks a lady and she answers "De rien !" What does she mean? | You're welcome | Nothing for you | Leave me alone | I've got nothing
exercise: Jake bumps into a man on a bus. What should he say? | Pardon ! | Excusez-moi, où sont les toilettes ? | De rien ! | Bon appétit !
exercise: Which phrase would you use to ask a lady politely where the toilets are? | Excusez-moi, madame, où sont les toilettes ? | Pardon, toi, où sont les toilettes ? | Salut, ma pote, où sont les toilettes, hein ? | Je veux les toilettes !
exercise: How does monsieur sound? | "muh-SYUH" | "mon-SEWER" | "MON-see-er" | "mon-SEER"
exercise: Theo wants a pancake. What is the polite way to ask? | Je voudrais une crêpe, s'il vous plaît. | Je veux une crêpe ! | Donne-moi une crêpe au chocolat, toi ! | Une crêpe, maintenant !
exercise: What does je voudrais mean? | I would like | I want | I need it now | I don't want
exercise: Molly's plate arrives first at lunch in Annecy. What should she do? | Wait for everyone and for someone to say bon appétit | Start eating at once, before her food gets cold and soggy | Say "Je veux manger !" and start | Leave the table until the others finish
exercise: Your teacher sneezes. What do you say? | À vos souhaits ! | À tes souhaits ! | Bon appétit ! | Bonne nuit !
exercise: What does someone answer after À vos souhaits ! ? | Merci ! | De rien ! | Pardon ! | Bonsoir !
exercise: What is the safe polite word for any grown-up woman today? | madame | mademoiselle | ma copine | maman
exercise: Who gets tu? | Your little cousin | The head teacher | A waiter in a café | A policeman you ask the way
test: Archie's friend mutters, "Bit much, mate," when Archie says tu to the teacher. Why? | Tu sounds too cheeky with a teacher; vous is polite | Tu is a rude word that must never be said to anyone | Tu is only for boys, and the teacher is a lady | Tu is only used in writing, never out loud
test: Emma's cousin nearly falls over laughing when she says vous to his dog. Why? | Vous to a puppy is like bowing to it; pets get tu | Vous is a rude word that you must never say to dogs in Quebec | Dogs in Montreal only understand English | She should have said madame to the dog
test: One kid answers Amir: "Tous les trois ?" ("All three of us?") What does Amir need to learn? | Vous is the you for a group, even of friends | Tu is the you for a group of three or more | French kids only play in pairs | You must always count people in French first
test: Poppy says "s'il te plaît" to the waiter. Her dad says, "Close, but he's not your mate." Which please was right? | s'il vous plaît | s'il te plaît | s'il tu plaît | s'il vous plaisez
test: Logan thinks "De rien !" is rude, like "nothing for you, kid." What's the truth? | It's a kind reply: "it was nothing, you're welcome" | He's right, it means "you get nothing" | It means "I didn't understand your English, sorry, try again" | It means "please go away now"
test: Jake goes "Sorry, bruv!" on a bus in Lille. ("Bruv" means mate.) What would a French kid say? | Pardon ! | Merci ! | Bonjour ! | Salut !
test: Charlotte says, "Why say madame? It sounds dead formal." ("Dead" means very.) What's the truth? | In France, bonjour, madame is normal and polite, even for kids | It is only for the Queen and very important people | It is old-fashioned, nobody in France says it any more, not even grans | It is rude, because it makes the lady sound old
test: Theo's gran says, "That's a bit grabby, my lover." ("My lover" is West Country for dear.) What was grabby? | Saying "Je veux une crêpe!" to the waiter | Saying "Je voudrais une crêpe" to the waiter | Saying "s'il vous plaît" twice | Saying "merci" when the crêpe came
test: Cooper is lowkey offended when his teacher says "À vos souhaits !" ("Lowkey" means a bit.) What did she mean? | Bless you | You are wet | Your voice is funny | Be quiet
test: Grace calls a young woman in a shop mademoiselle and asks, "Was that rude, like?" What's the best advice? | Use madame for grown-up women; it's always safe | Always use mademoiselle for young women, it's the polite rule | Call every woman ma chérie instead | Never speak to shop staff at all
test: ★★★ Your best friend AND your teacher are both sneezing. You speak to both together. What do you say? | À vos souhaits ! | À tes souhaits ! | À ton souhait ! | Bon appétit !
test: ★★★ Max writes three sentences. Which one is correct? | Maman, tu es fatiguée ? | Madame, tu veux un café ? | Les amis, tu voulez jouer ? | Monsieur, s'il te plaît !
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the use of the pronoun vous? | It is employed towards adults not intimately known and towards any group of persons | It is employed solely towards animals and young children | It is employed exclusively among close friends of equal age | It is considered discourteous in all circumstances and must be avoided entirely
exam: Identify the expression appropriate when a request is addressed to a waiter. | Je voudrais..., s'il vous plaît | Je veux..., s'il te plaît | Donne-moi... | Salut, je veux...
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the reply De rien. | It is a courteous acknowledgement of thanks, signifying "you are welcome" | It is a refusal, signifying "you shall receive nothing" | It is a request for repetition of what was said | It is a greeting employed solely in the evening
exam: It is given that a person has sneezed in the presence of a child, and that the person is an adult not intimately known. Determine the appropriate expression. | À vos souhaits | À tes souhaits | Bon appétit | De rien
